// Advanced questions, ccarf. Original items written against the published exam guide. Built from content/en by tools/build-en.js.
window.QUESTIONS_HARD = window.QUESTIONS_HARD || {};
window.QUESTIONS_HARD.ccarf = [
// ---- Domain 1 ----
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-01', d:1, o:0, s:1, q:"In a support agent's loop, after `process_refund` the model answers “the refund has been made”, but sometimes no refund has actually run. The trace shows the next API call being made without the tool being run and its result added to history. Which fix is correct?",
  c:["On `tool_use`, always run the tool and add a `tool_result` with the same `tool_use_id` to history before calling again", "State in the system prompt “report a refund only after checking the tool result”, to make the reports more accurate", "Always treat the turn after `process_refund` as `end_turn`, and change the flow to ask the user to confirm the result", "Force the refund tool alone through `tool_choice`, pinning the call so that the model cannot skip running it"],
  a:[0], e:"The basic loop is tool_use → run → add the tool_result to history → call again. Without the result, the model cannot know whether it ran, and neither a prompt nor tool_choice fixes that." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-02', d:1, o:0, s:3, q:"Which is the most robust way for a research agent to decide that it has finished?",
  c:["Continue until `stop_reason` is `end_turn`, with a maximum iteration count as a safety valve as well", "End when the words “research complete” appear in the response, or else run to the maximum iteration count", "End when two consecutive tool calls have identical arguments, and otherwise continue whatever the count", "End when a response is empty or has no tool call, waiting with no upper limit on the number of iterations"],
  a:[0], e:"stop_reason is the primary signal. Keywords in the body and matching arguments misfire, and having no cap allows a runaway." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-03', d:1, o:1, s:3, q:"In a coordinator-subagent setup, someone proposes changing the design so that “subagents talk to each other directly”. Which architectural judgment is soundest?",
  c:["Keep hub and spoke: handoffs between subagents go through the coordinator, so state is held in one place", "Allow direct links between subagents, and have the coordinator do nothing but assemble the final report", "Put the subagents in one shared context, so that all of them proceed while looking at the same history", "Merge the subagents into one, going back to a single agent that searches, analyzes and synthesizes in turn"],
  a:[0], e:"Hub and spoke is easy to trace and recover because the coordinator holds state and decisions. Direct links and shared context give up the benefit of isolation." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-04', d:1, o:1, s:3, q:"The synthesis subagent's output is missing evidence in places. Which design is a proper “iterative refinement loop”?",
  c:["The coordinator points out the gaps and has only those sections regenerated, in rounds with an upper limit", "Tell the synthesis subagent firmly in system to “always attach evidence”, and have it finish in one pass", "Whenever there is a gap, start over from the first search and rerun every one of the subagents again", "Delete the sections with gaps before output, and close the report with a note that “parts are unverified”"],
  a:[0], e:"A refinement loop passes specific feedback and fixes the affected part. It needs a cap. Redoing everything is costly, and deleting lowers quality." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-05', d:1, o:2, s:4, q:"When defining subagents in the Agent SDK, which configuration best suits an “Explorer that only searches files”?",
  c:["Limit `tools` in its `AgentDefinition` to Read, Grep and Glob", "Give it every tool the parent has, with “search only” written in system", "Give it no tools; the parent searches and it just summarizes the results", "Give it Bash only, free to combine `grep` and `find` to search and check"],
  a:[0], e:"Limiting tools to fit the role is the basic rule. A restriction in a prompt is probabilistic, and Bash alone is too broad a permission." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-06', d:1, o:2, s:3, q:"You want to spawn four search subagents in parallel, each on a different source. Which way of passing context is correct?",
  c:["Give each one only “the task, its assigned source, and the format to return”, without sharing the parent's history", "Copy the parent's history four times and pass it on, so each subagent can search with the full picture in mind", "Have one subagent go through the four sources in turn, with any parallelism done inside its tool calls", "Join the four subagents to a single shared session, set up so that each can see the others' search results"],
  a:[0], e:"A subagent has an isolated context and receives only the instructions it needs. Copying history wastes tokens, and sharing gives up the benefit of isolation." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-07', d:1, o:3, s:6, q:"In an extract → validate → register-downstream workflow, there was an incident where “data that failed validation got registered”. Which measure most surely prevents a repeat?",
  c:["Put a gate before the registration step: code checks the validation result and does not call registration on a failure", "State in the registration subagent's system prompt “never register any data that has failed the validation step”", "Merge validation and registration into the same agent, changing the design so that less context is overlooked", "Put an audit agent after registration, which deletes any invalid data it finds has been entered downstream"],
  a:[0], e:"A prerequisite gate is enforced in code. A prompt is probabilistic, and deleting afterwards takes the incident for granted." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-08', d:1, o:3, s:1, q:"On handoff from the support agent to a person, human agents complain “I cannot tell what is going on”. Which improvement fits best?",
  c:["On escalation, pass a fixed structure: summary, what was tried, customer sentiment and open issues", "Attach the full conversation as it is, so the human agent can read the parts needed and decide", "Add a step before escalation in which the model “tries just once more to resolve the case”", "Place a separate agent on the human side, to summarize the conversation again once received"],
  a:[0], e:"A handoff passes structured content. A full transcript is a burden to read, and another attempt or another summary does not solve the root problem." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-09', d:1, o:4, s:4, q:"Which implementation meets the requirement “stop `Bash` commands containing `rm -rf`” with an Agent SDK hook?",
  c:["A `PreToolUse` hook inspects the command string and, on a match, returns a denial so that it never runs", "A `PostToolUse` hook inspects the result and, if a deletion happened, restores the files from a backup", "Write “do not use `rm -rf`” in the system prompt, and use the hook only to log what gets executed", "Remove the `Bash` tool, and have every file operation done through `Write` and `Edit` alone"],
  a:[0], e:"Blocking before execution is PreToolUse. PostToolUse is after the fact, and a prompt is probabilistic. Removing Bash restricts more than the requirement asks." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-10', d:1, o:4, s:6, q:"Dates returned by MCP tools differ in format from source to source, and downstream breaks. Which hook-based response fits best?",
  c:["A `PostToolUse` hook normalizes dates to ISO 8601 before the result reaches the model", "A `PreToolUse` hook appends the instruction “return dates in ISO format” to the arguments", "Tell the model “convert dates to ISO in your output”, and do not use a hook for this at all", "Build parsers for every format into the downstream system, and pass formats through as received"],
  a:[0], e:"PostToolUse suits normalizing tool results. Instructions in arguments or about output are probabilistic, and handling every format downstream is heavy to maintain." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-11', d:1, o:5, s:4, q:"You are making a cross-cutting change with Claude Code: “replace the authentication scheme in every module”. Which decomposition approach fits best?",
  c:["First survey the full impact and make a plan, then split it into stages by authentication path, verifying as you go", "Open the files one by one in order and replace it, then run the tests all together once every file has been done", "Run a subagent per module at the same time, each replacing it by its own judgment, and integrate at the very end", "Fix the change as a prompt chain, settling each stage's inputs and outputs in advance, then run it mechanically"],
  a:[0], e:"A cross-cutting change needs dynamic decomposition that understands the dependencies. Per-file work and uncoordinated parallel decisions break consistency, and a fixed chain is weak against unknown dependencies." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-12', d:1, o:5, s:6, q:"For the process “classify the document type → extract by type → validate”, which is a sound reason to choose a (fixed) prompt chain?",
  c:["Each stage's inputs and outputs can be defined in advance and checked between stages, so it is highly predictable", "Making it fixed means a single API call is enough, which gives the lowest latency and cost that are possible", "Since the model does not have to work out the order of stages, the quality of its reasoning rises at every stage", "With the stages fixed, further document types can be handled later on without changing any of the prompts"],
  a:[0], e:"The benefits of a prompt chain are predictability and a check at each stage. The number of calls goes up, and adding a type does require changing prompts." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-13', d:1, o:6, s:2, q:"You want to use in today's session the design direction you worked out with Claude Code yesterday. Which method fits best?",
  c:["Resume yesterday's session with `--resume`, and continue with the context carried over", "Copy yesterday's output into today's first prompt, and begin in a brand new session", "Run `/compact`, open a new session, and wait for the summary to carry over by itself", "Append the full text of yesterday's conversation to CLAUDE.md, to be loaded each session"],
  a:[0], e:"A session is resumed with --resume. Pasting works but loses context. /compact operates within the current session, and appending a transcript to CLAUDE.md bloats it." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-14', d:1, o:6, s:3, q:"Partway through a long research session: “I want to try another hypothesis in parallel as well.” Which Agent SDK session management fits?",
  c:["Branch from the current state with `fork_session`, pursuing the other hypothesis without contaminating the original", "Try the other hypothesis right in the current session, and go back through the history to delete it if unwanted", "Open a new session, pass in a summary of the research so far, and try the other hypothesis in that one", "End the current session and resume it, then try the other hypothesis in the history that follows the resume"],
  a:[0], e:"Branching is a fork. History cannot be deleted. A new session with a summary works when results are not stale, but for branching, fork is the direct tool." },

// ---- Domain 2 ----
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-01', d:2, o:0, s:1, q:"There are two tools, `lookup_order` and `search_orders`, and the model often confuses them. What should you try first?",
  c:["Write “when to use, when not to use, example inputs” into each description, making the boundary clear", "Merge the two into one `orders` tool, with a `mode` argument switching between search and lookup", "Add a verification step downstream that detects a mix-up and has the model retry on any error", "Force the call with `tool_choice`, so that which of the two gets used is decided on the code side"],
  a:[0], e:"The tool description is the main cue for selection. Merging, or fixing the choice in code, comes later; adding verification does not treat the cause." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-02', d:2, o:0, s:4, q:"One `manage_repo` tool switches between “create branch, commit, create PR, merge” through an `action` argument. What is the design problem?",
  c:["Several responsibilities are mixed in one description and the input schema branches, so selection and argument accuracy suffer", "There are too few tools, so the model cannot grasp the whole picture of repository operations and so cannot make a selection", "Because the `action` argument is a string, the model will invent new operations on its own that then cannot be refused", "With a single tool, parallel calls become impossible, so a branch creation and a commit cannot be done at the same time"],
  a:[0], e:"Mixed responsibilities blur both the description and the input. Split it, and give each tool a clear description." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-03', d:2, o:1, s:1, q:"`process_refund` fails in the case “the order has already been refunded”. Which is the best way for the MCP tool to return this error?",
  c:["`isError: true`, category `business`, `isRetryable: false`, with the reason and the actions available next", "`isError: true`, category `transient`, `isRetryable: true`, so that the model goes on to retry the call", "`isError: false` with an empty result, letting the model read it as “succeeded” and carry on the chat", "Throw the exception as it is and stop the loop, showing the user nothing more than “system error”"],
  a:[0], e:"A business rule failure is business and not retryable. Include the reason and an alternative action. An empty result gets misread as success." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-04', d:2, o:1, s:6, q:"Which result design is correct for an extraction tool when “no matching document exists”?",
  c:["Return it as a normal empty result, not an error, stating plainly that there are “0 items”", "Return `isError: true` with category `validation`, prompting the model to review its input", "Return `isError: true` with category `transient`, so that it waits a little and then retries", "Return `null` as the result, and leave it to the model to interpret what that means"],
  a:[0], e:"Tell an empty result apart from an error. Zero items is a normal result. null is ambiguous, and treating it as an error invites pointless retries." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-05', d:2, o:2, s:3, q:"Giving the coordinator all 40 MCP tools led to more tool selection errors. Which response fits best?",
  c:["Split subagents by role, and pass each one only the tools it needs, scoped to that role", "Shorten the tool descriptions to cut total tokens, easing selection while keeping all 40", "Change to `tool_choice: any`, so it always calls some tool and hesitation is removed", "Fold the 40 descriptions into one long system prompt, and have tools called by number"],
  a:[0], e:"Keeping the tool count down and scoping by role is the basic rule. Shortening descriptions lowers accuracy, and any increases wrong picks." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-06', d:2, o:2, s:6, q:"“The structured result must always come back through the `extract_fields` tool.” Which `tool_choice` setting is correct?",
  c:["Specify `{type: \"tool\", name: \"extract_fields\"}`", "`{type: \"any\"}`, so that it must call one of the defined tools", "`{type: \"auto\"}`, steering the model by the tool description", "`{type: \"none\"}`, having it write the JSON in the body text"],
  a:[0], e:"Forcing a specific tool is type: tool with a name. any means “one of them”, auto is optional, and none means no tools." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-07', d:2, o:3, s:4, q:"Which placement is correct for the configuration of an MCP server the team shares?",
  c:["Define it in the project `.mcp.json`, with the API key read from each person's environment by `${VAR}` expansion", "Define it in each person's `~/.claude.json`, with the API key written in that same file and managed individually", "Define it in the project `.mcp.json`, with the API key written straight into it and shared through Git", "Write the connection steps in `CLAUDE.md`, and have each person register the server by hand at every start"],
  a:[0], e:"Project sharing is .mcp.json, with secrets through environment variable expansion. ~/.claude.json is personal scope." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-08', d:2, o:3, s:4, q:"An MCP server wants to offer “the list of specifications”. Which is a sound reason to expose it as a resource and not a tool?",
  c:["It is read-only reference data, to be taken in as content and not performed as an operation", "Resources execute faster than tools, so the latency requirement for showing a list is easier to meet", "Making it a resource removes the need for authentication settings, saving each person setup work", "There is an upper limit on how many tools can be registered, while resources have no such limit"],
  a:[0], e:"Resources expose data; tools are operations. Speed, authentication and limits are not reasons." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-09', d:2, o:4, s:4, q:"“List every file that uses the `UserService` class, and fix only those among them that do not use `async`.” Which use of the built-in tools is most efficient?",
  c:["List the usages with Grep, check the matching files with Read, and fix them with Edit", "Print every file with `cat` in Bash, and then rewrite them all together with Write", "List all `.ts` files with Glob, Read and judge each one, and overwrite with Write", "Replace across every file in one go with `sed` in Bash, without checking the result"],
  a:[0], e:"Grep for searching contents, Read for checking, Edit for partial fixes. Overwriting with Write, or sed with no check, invites accidents." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-10', d:2, o:4, s:2, q:"In which of these situations is `Write` the right tool and not `Edit`?",
  c:["Creating a new configuration file from scratch", "Fixing a single line in an existing function", "Adding one more import to an existing file", "Changing only the expected value in a test"],
  a:[0], e:"Write is for creating or replacing a whole file; Edit is for partial changes to an existing one. Using Write for a small change loses the other lines." },

// ---- Domain 3 ----
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-01', d:3, o:0, s:2, q:"Which pairing is correct for where these instructions go? “A: the language I prefer for answers”, “B: this repository's build steps”, “C: coding conventions for `packages/api/` only”.",
  c:["A: `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` / B: the project `CLAUDE.md` / C: `packages/api/CLAUDE.md`", "A: the project `CLAUDE.md` / B: `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` / C: `packages/api/CLAUDE.md`", "A: `packages/api/CLAUDE.md` / B: the project `CLAUDE.md` / C: `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`", "A: `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` / B: `packages/api/CLAUDE.md` / C: the project `CLAUDE.md`"],
  a:[0], e:"Personal preferences go in user scope, shared procedures in the project, and directory-specific rules in that directory." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-02', d:3, o:0, s:2, q:"The project CLAUDE.md has swollen to 600 lines, and important instructions are no longer being followed. Which reorganization fits best?",
  c:["Split it by topic into `.claude/rules/`, leaving in CLAUDE.md only the short points that apply everywhere", "Keep the 600 lines, but move the important instructions to the top in bold so that they are not skipped", "Move the whole text to `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, and leave the project's CLAUDE.md completely empty", "Delete CLAUDE.md altogether, and pass the instructions needed in the prompt at each new session"],
  a:[0], e:"Split with a modular structure (rules, @import), and narrow down what is always loaded. Bold type or moving the file does not cure the bloat." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-03', d:3, o:1, s:2, q:"You are making a slash command that “generates a PR description in the house format”. Which frontmatter is appropriate for `.claude/commands/pr-desc.md`?",
  c:["Limit `allowed-tools` to Bash(git …) and Read, and write an `argument-hint`", "Omit `allowed-tools` to allow every tool, explaining the arguments in prose at the end of the body", "Add `context: fork` to always run in a separate context, taking no arguments and fixing it in the body", "Pin `model` to the top tier, and set `allowed-tools` to only Write and Edit"],
  a:[0], e:"Allow a command only the tools it needs, and give an argument hint. fork is for long investigations, and Write/Edit are not needed to generate a description." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-04', d:3, o:1, s:2, q:"In which situation is it sound to set `context: fork` on a skill (`SKILL.md`)?",
  c:["An investigation that reads many files, where only a summary should return to the original session", "Just inserting a short template, where the original session's context is to be used exactly as it is", "Just answering the user's question in a single line, where no tool is going to be used at all", "A skill body of five lines or fewer, where the cost of loading it is small enough to ignore"],
  a:[0], e:"fork is the mechanism for doing heavy work without cluttering the original context. Short work, or work that should share the context, does not need it." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-05', d:3, o:2, s:2, q:"“Load the testing conventions only when `*.test.ts` files are being edited.” Which way of doing this is correct?",
  c:["Write `paths: [\"**/*.test.ts\"]` in the frontmatter of `.claude/rules/testing.md`", "Write in the project `CLAUDE.md`: “when editing `*.test.ts`, keep to the following conventions”", "Place a `tests/CLAUDE.md`, on the assumption that every test file lives in that directory", "Add `Edit(**/*.test.ts)` to `permissions.allow` in the `.claude/settings.json` file"],
  a:[0], e:"A path-specific rule uses paths (a glob) in YAML frontmatter. A conditional sentence in CLAUDE.md is always loaded, and assuming a directory does not fit a pattern." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-06', d:3, o:2, s:2, q:"Which correctly states the difference between a path-specific rule and a CLAUDE.md placed in a directory?",
  c:["A rule is conditioned on a file pattern (glob); a directory CLAUDE.md is determined by its location", "A rule is for personal use; a directory CLAUDE.md is the one that is used for team-wide settings", "A rule takes priority over the prompt; a directory CLAUDE.md is treated as reference information", "A rule is read just once at startup; a directory CLAUDE.md is reread again on every single turn"],
  a:[0], e:"paths in rules is a pattern; CLAUDE.md goes by hierarchy and location. The claims about scope, priority and loading frequency are wrong." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-07', d:3, o:3, s:2, q:"For which of these tasks is plan mode most justified?",
  c:["Changing the authentication scheme across several services, with the impact not yet understood", "Fixing a typo in a single file, and then confirming that the existing tests still pass", "Bumping one dependency package by a single version, and adding a line to the CHANGELOG", "Rewriting the steps in the README so that they match the current startup command"],
  a:[0], e:"Plan mode suits changes with a wide and unknown impact. A small change with a settled scope is fine with direct execution." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-08', d:3, o:3, s:4, q:"At the stage of working out “where to make the fix” in a large codebase, what is the advantage of using the Explore subagent?",
  c:["The results of reading many files stay out of the parent's context, and only the key points come back", "The subagent runs on a higher model than the parent does, so the accuracy of the exploration is higher", "Files found during the exploration can be fixed there and then, so fewer round trips are needed", "The subagent's results are cached, and so they can be used again in the next session as well"],
  a:[0], e:"Explore is read-only, and its advantage is keeping the parent's context clean. It cannot make fixes, and the claims about model and caching are wrong." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-09', d:3, o:4, s:2, q:"“The style of the generated code varies every time.” Which iterative refinement technique should you try first?",
  c:["Put 2 or 3 input and output examples of the style you want in CLAUDE.md, showing it concretely", "Say “same style as last time” at each generation, and wait for it to settle within the conversation", "Generate 10 times and adopt the commonest style, fixing any departures from it by hand afterwards", "Set the temperature to 0, so that the variation in output is held down mechanically by the sampling"],
  a:[0], e:"Input and output examples are the basic technique for reducing variation. Relying on the conversation, majority vote and temperature are not real solutions." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-10', d:3, o:4, s:2, q:"“Implementation starts while requirements are still vague, and there is a lot of rework.” Which refinement technique fits?",
  c:["Before implementing, have Claude ask questions about the requirements (an interview), and proceed from the answers", "Have the implementation generated in one go, and firm up the requirements afterwards while looking at the result", "Hand over the requirements as bullet points, accept no questions, and have it implement from one instruction", "Put the tests off and push the implementation ahead, adjusting it to the requirements once it is running"],
  a:[0], e:"Clear up ambiguity first with an interview. Generating everything at once, or forbidding questions, increases rework. Test-driven work is a separate, useful technique." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-11', d:3, o:5, s:5, q:"You want Claude Code to do a review in CI, with the result judged by machine. Which combination of options is correct?",
  c:["`-p` for non-interactive mode, with `--output-format json` and `--json-schema`", "`--resume` to continue the last review session, parsing the prose on stdout with a regular expression", "`-p` for non-interactive mode, detecting words like “problem” and “needs fixing” by regular expression", "Start in interactive mode, and have a person read the final message in the CI log to record pass or fail"],
  a:[0], e:"CI means non-interactive plus structured output plus a schema. Parsing prose, or having a person check, is not automation." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-12', d:3, o:5, s:5, q:"The same session that generated the code was asked to review it too, and it misses its own mistakes. Which CI design response fits?",
  c:["Run the review in a session (instance) independent of generation, giving it only the diff", "Give a further instruction in the same session, “review strictly”, to reduce what is missed", "Have the review repeated twice, with the second pass catching what the first one missed", "Raise the temperature for the review, so that findings come from more varied viewpoints"],
  a:[0], e:"Self-review carries over its own assumptions. Give only the diff to an independent instance." },

// ---- Domain 4 ----
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-01', d:4, o:0, s:5, q:"With automated review in CI, “there are so many false positives that developers have started ignoring the findings”. What is the first response in prompt design?",
  c:["State explicitly what to flag (null dereference, unhandled exceptions) and what not to flag (style)", "Limit findings to 3 per run and hold the rest over to the next review, to lighten the load on developers", "Say “flag only what you are sure about”, and leave the decision on what to flag up to the model", "Narrow the review to PRs with a large diff only, and take the small PRs out of scope altogether"],
  a:[0], e:"Explicit criteria help both precision and false positives. A cap on the count, or “if you are sure”, is no substitute for criteria." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-02', d:4, o:0, s:1, q:"The support agent proposes refunds even for enquiries that are “not eligible for a refund”. Which prompt response fits?",
  c:["Write the conditions for and against a refund as specific criteria, and define what to do when unsure", "Write “be careful with refunds” in system, urging it to hold back from proposing them too readily", "Have every enquiry that contains a refund-related word escalated to a person without exception", "Add a sentence that has it ask itself “is this really all right?” before it proposes any refund"],
  a:[0], e:"Define explicit criteria and the action for unclear cases. A vague caution or escalating everything costs either precision or efficiency." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-03', d:4, o:1, s:6, q:"You added three few-shot examples, but accuracy rose only for documents like the examples and nothing else changed. What should you do next?",
  c:["Include boundary examples as well as typical ones (empty fields, multiple matches, differing formats)", "Raise the number of examples to 15, so that as many document shapes as possible are covered", "Take out every example and have it judge from instructions alone, to avoid overfitting to examples", "Cut down to one example per document type, lowering the total in order to save on tokens"],
  a:[0], e:"Variety in the examples (boundary cases) is what helps generalization. Composition matters more than number, and removing them is a step back." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-04', d:4, o:1, s:5, q:"Which is correct advice for writing few-shot examples?",
  c:["Keep the examples in one format, do not contradict the instructions, and label unwanted output as a “bad example”", "Use real data for the examples exactly as it is, leaving any personal information in for the sake of accuracy", "The more examples there are the higher the accuracy, so put in as many as will fit up to the context limit", "Generate the examples with a different model from the one used in production, to avoid bias toward one model"],
  a:[0], e:"Consistency, agreement with the instructions, and labeling good and bad examples. Leaving in personal data and maximizing volume are wrong." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-05', d:4, o:2, s:6, q:"`invoice_date` was made required in the extraction schema, and documents with no date get an invented one. Which design fix is correct?",
  c:["Make `invoice_date` nullable, so “absent” can be expressed and the gap can be detected downstream", "Keep `invoice_date` required, with a convention of entering `\"1970-01-01\"` when there is none", "Make `invoice_date` a free-text string, and accept whatever explanation the model puts in it", "Write “do not make up a date when there is none” in system, and leave the schema as it is"],
  a:[0], e:"Required fields invite fabrication. Make it nullable to express absence. Sentinel values and prompt-only instructions are fragile." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-06', d:4, o:2, s:6, q:"`document_type` was made an enum (invoice / receipt / contract). Which design is correct for when “a document that fits none of them” arrives in production?",
  c:["Add `other` to the enum; a document that does not fit goes there and is routed to separate handling", "Keep the enum as it is; when nothing fits, have the model choose the closest type and carry on", "Drop the enum for free text, so that some type name or other is returned for any document at all", "Reject documents that do not fit at the input stage, and operate without touching the schema"],
  a:[0], e:"Give an enum an “other” escape route. Forcing a choice increases misclassification." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-07', d:4, o:3, s:6, q:"Validating an extraction raised the error “the total does not equal the sum of the line items”. Which way of passing the retry is most effective?",
  c:["Have it re-extract with the specifics of the mismatch (the total and the line values) attached", "Resend the same prompt with nothing changed, and wait for sampling variation to fix it", "Raise the temperature and resend, so that a different extraction result is produced", "Return it with just the total left empty, and have it calculated downstream instead"],
  a:[0], e:"A retry with the error attached is the basic rule. Resending the same input, or changing temperature, is guesswork." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-08', d:4, o:3, s:6, q:"The same document has raised the same validation error three times in a row. Which design decision is correct?",
  c:["Stop retrying and send it to human review, recording the detected pattern for use in improvement", "Raise the retry limit to 10, and carry on with the error attached until validation finally passes", "Ignore the error and adopt the last result, passing it downstream marked as having been validated", "Drop the document from processing, log nothing but “extraction failed”, and move on to the next"],
  a:[0], e:"Recognize a retry that cannot help, hand over to a person, and record the pattern. Endless retries, ignoring the error and silent discarding are all wrong." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-09', d:4, o:4, s:6, q:"You want to move a nightly extraction of 50,000 documents to the Message Batches API. Choose two correct design assumptions.",
  c:["Attach a `custom_id` to each request, to match results back to the source files", "Tool call round trips are not possible, so extraction must complete in one call", "Batches finish within minutes, so downstream can keep the same SLA as synchronous", "Batch costs more than synchronous, so switch to synchronous on days with few items"],
  a:[0,1], e:"Match by custom_id; multi-turn tool round trips are not possible. Completion can take up to 24 hours, and the price is 50% lower." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-10', d:4, o:4, s:6, q:"Which of these jobs does NOT suit the Message Batches API?",
  c:["An extraction shown on screen right after a user uploads a file", "Extracting together the documents that built up during the night", "A monthly batch job that reclassifies every one of the contracts", "Running a regression test against an evaluation set of 2,000 items"],
  a:[0], e:"Work that needs an immediate answer does not suit batch. Work done in bulk with time to spare does." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-11', d:4, o:5, s:5, q:"You want a review from three angles: security, performance and readability. Which multi-pass design fits best?",
  c:["Review each angle in an independent instance, then merge the results and remove the duplicates", "Write all three angles into one prompt, and have every finding raised in a single review pass", "Have one session review three times in a row, each later pass building on the earlier results", "Use a different model for each angle, and adopt only the results that come from the top model"],
  a:[0], e:"An independent instance per angle reduces oversights. Combining them in one pass thins the review, and consecutive passes are pulled along by earlier results." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-12', d:4, o:5, s:5, q:"Which is the soundest reason why quality plateaus when “generate → self-review → fix” runs in the same instance?",
  c:["It carries over the assumptions and readings from generation, so it misses the same blind spots again", "Self-review consumes a great many tokens, so the context reaches its limit and the run then stops", "Within the same instance the temperature is fixed, so it is unable to generate any different output", "As the number of self-review rounds goes up, the model starts to forget its own earlier results"],
  a:[0], e:"The limit of self-review is shared assumptions. An independent instance is the answer." },

// ---- Domain 5 ----
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-01', d:5, o:0, s:1, q:"In a long support conversation, by the end the model forgets the “order number” and “customer constraints” that came up early on. Which response fits best?",
  c:["Hold the case facts (order number, constraints, decisions) as structure, and present them at the start or end of each turn", "Keep the entire conversation history, and write in system “do not forget the information from early in the conversation”", "Switch the conversation to a new session every 10 turns, carrying over nothing but the last few turns each time", "Switch to a model with a larger context, so that there is room enough even with the whole of the history included"],
  a:[0], e:"Keep and present case facts. The middle gets buried (lost in the middle), so simply keeping history is not enough." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-02', d:5, o:0, s:1, q:"Each `lookup_order` result is 8 KB of JSON, and the context swells within a few turns. Which response fits?",
  c:["Trim the tool result to the fields needed (status, amount, date) before it goes into history", "Put the result in as it is, and raise `max_tokens` to make sure of room for the output", "Compress the result to base64 in history, and have the model expand it only when needed", "Limit tool calls to two per conversation, so that the swelling is kept in check that way"],
  a:[0], e:"Trimming tool output is the basic rule. max_tokens is the output side, compressed data cannot be read, and a call limit harms function." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-03', d:5, o:1, s:1, q:"The customer said “I want to talk to a person”, yet the agent kept trying to resolve the case. Which escalation design is correct?",
  c:["An explicit request for a person is escalated at once, with no attempt at resolution in between", "Try to resolve it just once, and escalate only if the customer still asks for a person after that", "Estimate the customer's sentiment, and escalate once the level of anger passes a set threshold", "Put the first-contact resolution target first, and treat escalation as the very last resort"],
  a:[0], e:"A request for a human is honored at once. Inserting an attempt or a sentiment estimate amounts to ignoring the request." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-04', d:5, o:1, s:1, q:"A search by order number returned three hits. Which is the best way to resolve the ambiguity?",
  c:["Show identifying details of the three (date, amount), and ask the customer which one it is", "Pick the most recent one and proceed, waiting for the customer to say so if it is wrong", "Apply the same handling to all three, and tell the customer the results all together", "Since it is ambiguous, break off the handling and escalate the case to a human agent"],
  a:[0], e:"Multiple matches are resolved by asking. Guessing or handling all of them causes errors, and escalating at once is unnecessary." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-05', d:5, o:2, s:3, q:"One of the search subagents failed because its source was down. Which way of telling the coordinator fits?",
  c:["Return as structure what failed, why, and what is left uncovered, and leave an annotation in the report", "Hide the failure and return an empty result, leaving the coordinator to make it up quietly from other sources", "Throw the exception as it is, stopping the coordinator and having the whole job started again from the top", "In place of the failed source, return a result that has been filled in from the model's own knowledge"],
  a:[0], e:"Structured error context and a coverage annotation. Hiding, halting and filling in are all wrong." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-06', d:5, o:2, s:3, q:"Which is the correct reason to prefer “local recovery” in multi-agent error propagation?",
  c:["The place nearest the failure holds the context, and can judge a retry or an alternative most cheaply", "The coordinator is designed so that it cannot handle errors, so they have to be dealt with in subagents", "If recovery is done locally the error need not be recorded, which cuts down the volume of the logs", "Local recovery always succeeds, so the mechanism for telling the level above can simply be left out"],
  a:[0], e:"Recovery close to the failure is cheap and accurate. It still needs recording, and on failure it is passed up as structure." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-07', d:5, o:3, s:4, q:"In a 200,000-line codebase you need to understand “the flow of payment processing”. Which way of managing context fits best?",
  c:["Leave exploration to subagents, write findings to a scratchpad or manifest, and return only key points to the parent", "Open the files that look relevant one by one with Read, taking the full text of each into the parent's context", "Run `/compact` frequently, reading on while continually summarizing the content of the files already opened", "Change to a model that can take the entire codebase in one single prompt, and have it read everything at once"],
  a:[0], e:"Subagents plus a scratchpad or manifest protect the parent's context. Reading everything, or leaning on compact, has limits." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-08', d:5, o:3, s:4, q:"When is the right time to use `/compact`?",
  c:["When a stretch of investigation has ended, to summarize the findings before the next task", "Every time one file is opened, so that the context is always held down to the minimum", "Right after the session starts, in order to have the content of CLAUDE.md summarized", "Whenever an error appears, to wipe the history of the error and then try once again"],
  a:[0], e:"compact is a summary at a natural break. Too often and it loses context; right at the start, or to erase errors, is the wrong purpose." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-09', d:5, o:4, s:6, q:"For human review of extractions: “reviewing everything is impossible, and random sampling misses the important errors”. Which design fits best?",
  c:["Stratify by document type and confidence, and sample the low-confidence and high-risk strata more heavily", "Sample 5% uniformly at random from the whole set, and accept whatever is missed as statistical error", "Review only the stratum with the highest confidence, to evidence the accuracy and then apply it to all", "Stop reviewing, add more validation rules, and substitute mechanical checks for the human review"],
  a:[0], e:"Stratified sampling, weighted by type and risk. Uniform sampling, or high confidence only, misses more." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-10', d:5, o:4, s:6, q:"Extraction confidence is output as “one number per document”. What should be improved from the standpoint of calibration?",
  c:["Make it confidence per field, showing reviewers which items are the uncertain ones", "Change confidence from 0–1 to an integer from 0 to 100, to make it easier to read", "Stop outputting confidence, and decide on review by whether validation errors exist", "Make the document confidence the average of the fields, not the lowest field value"],
  a:[0], e:"Field-level confidence decides where review should focus. Changing the scale or averaging adds no information." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-11', d:5, o:5, s:3, q:"A report that synthesizes several sources is not trusted, because “which claim came from which source” cannot be told. Which design fix is correct?",
  c:["Map each claim to its source and date, and annotate the fact wherever there is a conflict", "List the sources consulted together at the end of the report, leaving out the link to claims", "Narrow the sources to the single most credible one before synthesis, so no conflict can arise", "Delete both sides of any conflicting claim, and include only what all the sources agree on"],
  a:[0], e:"Provenance is kept per claim. A list at the end does not show the link, and cutting sources or deleting conflicts loses information." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-12', d:5, o:5, s:3, q:"Two sources give different values for the same metric (a 2024 edition and a 2026 edition). Which handling during synthesis fits best?",
  c:["Show both values with their dates, and note the reason the newer one was adopted", "Show only the newer one, leaving the older value out so as to avoid confusion", "Take the average of the two values, and present it as “an approximate figure”", "As it cannot be judged which one is correct, leave that metric out altogether"],
  a:[0], e:"Keep the dates and state the uncertainty. Silently dropping one, averaging, or omitting the metric is wrong." },
// ---- Added from study notes ----
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-15', d:1, o:0, q:"Reviewing an agentic loop implementation. Which statement is **wrong**?",
  c:["If a cap on iterations (say 20) is made the main stop condition, there is no need to look at `stop_reason`", "If `stop_reason` is `tool_use`, run the requested tool, add the result to history and send the request again", "A cap on iterations is placed as a safety device, but the loop ends on `stop_reason` becoming `end_turn`", "Text can come back together with a tool request, so the presence of text is not a signal of completion"],
  a:[0], e:"A cap is a safety device, not the stop condition. A cap alone either cuts the work off partway or spins needlessly." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-1-16', d:1, o:5, q:"A large code review covering 45 files was shown in a single prompt, and the findings came out shallow and contradictory. Which response fits **best**?",
  c:["Attention is being diluted. Split into local per-file analysis passes and a cross-file integration pass (data flow and the like)", "The context limit is the cause. Switch to a model with a larger window, and show it all again in the same single prompt", "The model's confidence is low. Instruct it to give “high-confidence findings only”, which cuts down the shallow ones", "The review instruction is vague. Add the instruction “be conservative and do not contradict yourself”, and rerun it"],
  a:[0], e:"A predictable multi-aspect review is a prompt chain (a fixed sequential pipeline). An investigation you cannot see ahead in calls for dynamic, adaptive decomposition." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-11', d:2, o:1, q:"Of these four MCP tool error responses, choose the **two** that are **appropriate**.",
  c:["`{isError:true, errorCategory:\"business\", isRetryable:false, message:\"The refund deadline has passed\"}`", "`{isError:true, errorCategory:\"transient\", isRetryable:true, message:\"Inventory service timed out\"}`", "`{isError:true, message:\"The operation failed\"}` (no category, no retryability, and one uniform wording for every failure)", "`{isError:false, result:[]}` (when the inventory service was unreachable, the same empty success as “nothing matched”)"],
  a:[0,1], e:"A uniform “it failed” gives nothing to choose a recovery from. Returning an access failure as an empty success gets misread as “nothing matched”." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-2-12', d:2, o:3, q:"You are adding a Jira integration the team shares to Claude Code. Which setup is **most appropriate**?",
  c:["A community Jira MCP server written in `.mcp.json` under version control, the token expanded from the environment as `${JIRA_TOKEN}`", "A Jira MCP server built in-house that each person registers in `~/.claude.json`, with the token written straight into that file", "A community Jira MCP server that each person registers in `~/.claude.json`, with the token written in `.mcp.json` and shared", "A Jira MCP server built in-house and placed under version control in `.mcp.json`, with the token itself included in the file"],
  a:[0], e:"For standard integrations use an existing community server; build your own only for team-specific workflows. .mcp.json is shared with the team, ~/.claude.json is personal or experimental. Credentials go through environment variable expansion and are never committed." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-13', d:3, o:0, q:"Which way of writing CLAUDE.md goes **against** good practice?",
  c:["Writing every line you want kept with “always” or “never” for emphasis, so all carry equal weight", "Writing “new API routes go in the given directory, one route per file”, not “follow best practices”", "Writing “use named exports, not default exports” in place of “do not use default exports”", "Treating Claude's mistakes as bug reports against CLAUDE.md, and deleting lines that cannot be justified"],
  a:[0], e:"Emphasis is a budget. It works only when its surroundings are quiet, so keep it to the 2 or 3 things that would really hurt if broken." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-3-14', d:3, o:3, q:"Which of these pairings of task and way of working is **not appropriate**?",
  c:["A single-file bug fix with a clear stack trace → investigate in plan mode, get approval, then implement", "Restructuring microservices → investigate in plan mode, then implement by direct execution once decided", "Adding one validation condition on a date → direct execution, as it is simple with a clear scope", "Choosing among integration approaches with different infrastructure needs → explore safely in plan mode"],
  a:[0], e:"A simple change with a clear scope is faster by direct execution. Plan mode is for large changes, several reasonable approaches, or architectural decisions. Combine them: investigate in plan mode, implement the settled approach directly." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-13', d:4, o:2, q:"Choose **two** extraction schema designs that keep the model from fabricating items the document does not contain.",
  c:["Make a field nullable when the source document may not have the information", "Include `\"unclear\"` in the enum as the place for the ambiguous cases to go", "Make every field required, and reject empty output as a syntax error", "Write normalization rules for variant spellings in the schema, not in the prompt"],
  a:[0,1], e:"Adding \"other\" with a detail string field to an enum lets you widen it later. Format normalization rules go in the prompt, used together with a strict schema." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-4-14', d:4, o:4, q:"There are four jobs: a weekly audit, overnight test generation, a pre-merge check and an overnight report. Which one **should use the synchronous API**?",
  c:["The pre-merge check (work waits for the result)", "The weekly audit (can wait; nothing is blocked)", "Overnight test generation (can wait; nothing blocked)", "The overnight report (can wait; nothing is blocked)"],
  a:[0], e:"Work that can wait goes to the Message Batches API (50% lower cost, up to 24 hours, no SLA). Work that waits on the result before moving on uses the synchronous API." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-13', d:5, o:1, q:"A customer asks you to “match another company's price”. Your policy covers only price adjustments within your own site. Which response fits **best**?",
  c:["Escalate. The policy says nothing at all about what the customer is asking for (a policy gap)", "Resolve it autonomously by applying the own-site price adjustment rule. A similar rule is enough", "Acknowledge the frustration and politely decline to match. What is not in policy gets refused", "Assess the customer's confidence and sentiment, and escalate only where the anger is strong"],
  a:[0], e:"The proper triggers are: a person was asked for, a policy exception or gap, or no progress. Sentiment and self-reported confidence are unreliable." },
{ id:'h-ccarf-5-14', d:5, o:5, q:"In synthesizing a research report, which handling is **wrong** in terms of preserving provenance and treating uncertainty?",
  c:["Putting financial data, news and technical findings all into one bulleted format for readability", "Having subagents output source URL, document name and relevant excerpt as a mapping that is kept", "Including publication or collection dates in structured output, so timing is not read as conflict", "Giving established and contested findings separate sections, keeping the sources' wording and method"],
  a:[0], e:"Fit the form to the content: financial data as a table, news as prose, technical findings as a structured list. Do not convert everything into the same format." },
];
